  faqDishImage: {fileID: 0}
  faqEntries:
  - type: 1
    text: "Do you know why is it called \u201Casado\u201D if the chicken is not roasted?"
    answer: "In Spanish, \u201Casado\u201D means roasted. Philippine kitchens of
      the colonial era could not easily sustain oven roasting, so the name stayed
      while the method shifted to braising. The word remained, the technique adapted."
  - type: 1
    text: 'Do you know why is chicken asado considered a Kapampangan dish? '
    answer: 'Pampanga was the Spanish colonial culinary heartland of the Philippines.
      Kapampangan families perfected the sweet-savory-acid balance that defines the
      regional canon, and the dish became a Sunday and feast-day signature of the
      province. '
  - type: 1
    text: 'Do you know why is the tomato considered an exotic ingredient historically? '
    answer: 'The tomato is a New World plant, native to Mesoamerica. It reached the
      Philippines only in the sixteenth century through the Manila Galleon trade
      from Acapulco. Without the galleon trade, there would be no tomato in Filipino
      cooking. '
